  <section>
    <h2>Dispensaries in Millinocket</h2>
    <h3>Magic City Med Shop</h3>
    <p><strong>Address:</strong> 166 Central St, Millinocket, ME 04462<br>
    <strong>Phone:</strong> (207) 261-1004<br>
    <strong>Hours:</strong> confirm before visiting<br>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Medical (MMMP)</p>
    <p>Magic City Med Shop is a medical dispensary in Millinocket and a practical first stop for patients heading to or from Baxter State Park and Mount Katahdin. The "Magic City" name is a nod to Millinocket's industrial-era nickname from the Great Northern Paper Company mill era. <a href="https://magiccitymedshop.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">magiccitymedshop.com</a></p>

    <h3>West Branch Wellness</h3>
    <p><strong>Address:</strong> 256 Penobscot Ave, Millinocket, ME 04462<br>
    <strong>Phone:</strong> (207) 320-8464<br>
    <strong>Hours:</strong> Mon–Fri ~10am–7pm (confirm before visiting)<br>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Medical (MMMP) — part of Vibes Cannabis Co.</p>
    <p>West Branch Wellness is a medical cannabis caregiver named for the West Branch of the Penobscot River, which flows through Millinocket and the surrounding Penobscot highlands. The dispensary emphasizes a wellness-oriented product mix, including tinctures, topicals, and edibles, alongside standard flower and concentrates.</p>

    <h3>Outer Limits Med Shop</h3>
    <p><strong>Address:</strong> 200 Iron Bridge Road, Millinocket, ME 04462<br>
    <strong>Phone:</strong> (207) 447-6870<br>
    <strong>Hours:</strong> confirm before visiting<br>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Medical (MMMP)</p>
    <p>Outer Limits Med Shop is a small, family-owned medical dispensary serving the Millinocket area. Its name and branding lean into the outdoor-recreation identity of the Katahdin region. For hikers, snowmobilers, and seasonal visitors to the Baxter State Park corridor, it is a familiar stop.</p>
  </section>

  <Callout title="Katahdin Gateway" type="passion">
    Millinocket sits on ME-11/ME-157 about 20 miles from the Abol Bridge and Roaring Brook trailheads on the northeast side of Baxter State Park — the side used by most hikers summiting Katahdin and by northbound thru-hikers finishing the Appalachian Trail. The three-store dispensary cluster means the gateway town is unusually well-served, and the spring–fall hiker season brings a steady stream of visitors making the Bangor-to-Millinocket run.
  </Callout>

  <section>
    <h2>Millinocket's Market Context</h2>
    <p>Millinocket was built in the late 19th century around the Great Northern Paper Company mill, which for nearly a century was the largest paper mill in the world. The mill's closure in 2008 triggered a long period of population decline and economic restructuring. The town has since reinvented itself as the southern gateway to Mount Katahdin and Baxter State Park, with a growing outdoor-recreation economy anchored by hiking, snowmobiling, ATV riding, and river-based tourism on the West Branch of the Penobscot.</p>
    <p>For cannabis, Millinocket is a stable three-store market. The three operators — Magic City Med Shop, West Branch Wellness, and Outer Limits Med Shop — serve a population of about 4,400 plus a wide rural catchment (East Millinocket, Medway, Patten, Sherman) and a heavy seasonal flow of Katahdin-bound visitors. The established storefronts are primarily medical (MMMP). It is one of the better-served dispensary markets in interior Penobscot County for its population size.</p>
    <p>Operators evaluating the Penobscot highlands corridor should treat Millinocket as the Katahdin-region anchor, with Bangor (~70 mi south) and Lincoln (~40 mi east) as the next nearest dispensary clusters. The Millinocket market is mature and stable, with limited room for additional entries unless they bring a meaningfully different product mix or service model.</p>
  </section>


